":\"submitted\"}\n6. ✅ Idempotency support with proper replay detection\n7. ✅ Uses Idempotency-Key header validation from protocol.Item\n8. ✅ ActionRequest entries with \"reopen.v5\" operation\n9. ✅ Concurrent request handling with guarded RLS\n\nLet me verify the protocol.Item implementation handles the role-based access correctly. Looking at protocol.go, I see it checks `u.Role != role`, so by passing \"requester\" as the role, only requesters can access this endpoint. Approvers will get 403. ✅\n\nNow let me verify the fingerprint logic is correct. The Fingerprint function sorts IDs and combines them with the operation. For a single-item action, I'm using `[]string{id, reason}` to create the hash. This ensures that:\n- Same ID + same reason + same key = replay\n- Same ID + different reason + same key = 409\n- Different ID + same key = 409\n\nActually, looking at the requirements more carefully, it says \"同一 actor 以同一 key 对同一采购单、同一原因重放时\" - same actor with same key for same purchase and same reason should be replayed. My current implementation hashes `[id, reason]`, but the Fingerprint function sorts these strings. This might not be ideal. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, re-reading the Fingerprint function, it sorts the IDs. In this single-item case, I'm passing `[id, reason]` as the \"IDs\", which will be sorted. This could cause issues if reason happens to sort before or after id unpredictably. Let me fix this by using a clearer approach - concatenating id and reason in a deterministic way:\nNow let's rebuild to verify:\nExcellent! Let me verify the final implementation one more time:\nPerfect! Let me create a summary of the implementation:\n\n## Implementation Summary\n\nI have successfully implemented the reopen Action (POST /purchases/{id}/reopen) for the purchase service. Here's what was added:\n\n### Files Created/Modified\n\n**1. `/internal/candidate/reopen.go` (new file)**\n- Implements the `newReopen` function that returns a `protocol.ItemHandler`\n- Handles single-target purchase reopen requests with full idempotency support\n\n**2. `/internal/candidate/approval.go` (modified)**\n- Updated comment to indicate both batch and single-target actions are here\n- Registered the reopen endpoint via `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", ...)`\n- Chains the new endpoint with the existing bulk-approve/bulk-reject routes\n\n### Key Features\n\n✅ **Route**: POST /purchases/{id}/reopen\n✅ **Request Body**: {\"reason\":\"...\"} with validation of 1-200 byte reason\n✅ **Authentication**: Only authenticated requesters via Bearer token\n✅ **Authorization**: \n   - Only requesters can call (403 for approvers)\n   - Only own purchases in current tenant (400 for others)\n   - Only rejected status purchases (400 otherwise)\n\n✅ **Idempotency**:\n   - Validates Idempotency-Key header (1-128 alphanumeric characters)\n   - Detects replays: same key + same purchase + same reason = returns replayed response with Idempotency-Replayed: true\n   - Different purchase with same key = 409\n   - Different reason with same key = 409\n   - Concurrent requests: advisory lock ensures only one succeeds\n\n✅ **Status Transition**: rejected → submitted\n\n✅ **Response**: 200 {\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}\n\n✅ **Audit Trail**: \n   - ActionRequest table: operation=\"reopen.v5\", with requestHash and response\n   - ApprovalAudit table: records transition with reason field\n\n✅ **Database Consistency**: All writes in single transaction, RLS policies enforced by guarded runtime\n\n✅ **Error Handling**: \n   - 400 for validation errors or ineligible purchases\n   - 401 for missing/invalid authentication\n   - 403 for wrong role\n   - 409 for conflicts (same key, different request)\n   - 503 for database failures with automatic retry capability\n\n✅ **Conventions**: Follows existing patterns from bulk-approve and bulk-reject implementations\n\nThe implementation is complete, compiles successfully, and maintains all existing APIs."
